# Component file split

For components with real state or pure logic, split the folder. Keep simple atoms/molecules as a single `component-name.tsx` (+ stories).

Types follow `.agents/rules/types.mdc` — a `*.types.ts` file exists **only** for types **another file imports** (single-file types stay inline; don't declare result/return types, prefer inference). A component with no cross-file types has **no** `*.types.ts`. Related local state (≥3 fields that change together) → `useReducer` per `.agents/rules/state.mdc`.

```
component-name/
  component-name.tsx              # JSX + event handlers (onPress / onClick wiring)
  component-name.types.ts         # ONLY if another file imports these types (Props a parent builds, shared shapes); else omit — see types.mdc
  use-component-name.ts           # state, derived values, effects
  use-component-name.animations.ts # ALL animation handling (shared/reanimated values, timings, gestures) — see below
  component-name.helpers.ts       # pure functions (no React, no hooks)
  component-name.stories.tsx
  *.test.ts(x)                    # co-located; one suite per file above
```

## What goes where

| File | Owns | Does not own |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `*.tsx` | layout, styles, a11y attrs, **event handlers** that call hook setters / props | business state machines, **animation values** |
| `use-*.ts` | `useState` / `useReducer` / `useEffect`, derived flags, computed getters (see `state.mdc`) | `onPress` / `onClick` handlers, **animation values** |
| `use-*.animations.ts` | **all animation handling** — animated/shared values, timing/spring configs, gesture handlers, the animated `style`/props the component spreads onto its views | non-animation state (that's `use-*.ts`), pure non-animated transforms |
| `*.helpers.ts` | pure transforms / lookups / label builders | React, hooks, side effects |
| `*.types.ts` | types **another file imports** (a `Props` a parent builds, shared shapes) — created only then | private/single-file types (keep inline), declared result/return types (infer), runtime logic (see `types.mdc`) |

## Rules of thumb

- **Handlers stay in the component.** Hook exposes state + setters (and maybe small derived helpers like `itemState(id)`); the component wires taps/clicks.
- **Animation lives in its own hook — `use-<component-name>.animations.ts`.** Any component that animates (Reanimated `useSharedValue`/`useAnimatedStyle`, `Animated.Value`, timings, springs, gesture-driven motion) puts **all** of that in a dedicated `use-<name>.animations.ts`, separate from `use-<name>.ts` (interaction/data state). It returns the animated `style`/props (and any imperative controls) for the component to spread; keep it free of business/data state. A component with **no** animation has no `.animations.ts` file. Animation is a presentation concern — never mix it into the data-state hook or the component body.
- **Helpers must be pure** — easy to unit-test without RTL.
- **Don't invent a hook** for a dumb presentational atom.
- This is **UI co-location**, not the data layer. Data still follows `Component → Hook → Service → DAO` (see `hooks-service-dao.mdc`). A `use-matching` here is local interaction state; a `use-lessons` in `@helsoft/hooks` wraps a service. Local related state still follows `state.mdc`.
